### Why category theory is useful (no abstract nonsense)\
\
**It is a "type system for thought."**\
\
| Instead of... | You think... |\
|---|---|\
| "What is inside this vector space?" | "What maps into or out of it?" |\
| "This neural net has 12 layers" | "This is 12 morphisms composed" |\
| "Does this quantum gate work?" | "Do the types (Hilbert space dimensions) match?" |\
\
The only rule that matters: **you can only connect arrows if the types match.** Codomain of f must equal domain of g. Everything else (associativity, identity) is just making sure composition behaves like you expect.\
\
---\
\
### How to use it correctly\
\
1. **Pick your objects** = the "types" of things you care about (integers, quantum states, tensors, datasets).\
2. **Pick your morphisms** = the valid transformations between those types. A morphism `f: A \uc0\u8594  B` means "I know how to turn an A into a B."\
3. **Check composition**: `g \uc0\u8728  f` only exists if `f` outputs exactly what `g` inputs.\
4. **Verify the two laws** (the widget does this for you):\
   - **Associativity**: `(h \uc0\u8728  g) \u8728  f` must equal `h \u8728  (g \u8728  f)` \'97 the order of grouping cannot matter.\
   - **Identity**: There must exist an `id_A` for every object A such that `f \uc0\u8728  id_A = f` and `id_B \u8728  f = f`.\
\
If those two checks pass for your collection of objects and morphisms, congratulations \'97 you have a category. You can now apply every theorem in category theory to your system.\
\
---\
\
### How to check this with an AI\
\
The widget above **is** the AI checker. But if you want to verify a real system (e.g., your neural network or a quantum circuit), do this:\
\
1. **Model your system as a graph**: Nodes = objects (tensor shapes, state spaces). Edges = morphisms (layers, gates, operations).\
2. **Ask the AI**: "Verify that for every composition `g \uc0\u8728  f` in this graph, the output type of `f` matches the input type of `g`." This is a **functoriality check**.\
3. **Ask the AI**: "Verify associativity for all triples of sequential operations." In neural nets this is trivial (layer stacking is associative). In quantum circuits with controlled operations, it is not \'97 and this is where bugs hide.\
4. **Ask the AI**: "Does this construction satisfy a universal property?" (product, coproduct, limit). If yes, you get guarantees for free \'97 e.g., if your attention mechanism is a categorical product, certain optimality proofs apply automatically.\
\
---\
\
### The one-sentence summary\
\
> Category theory is the discipline of **drawing boxes and arrows correctly** \'97 and once you do, the math guarantees that complex systems built from simple pieces will not break in surprising ways.\
\
Play with the widget. Try breaking associativity (e.g., define a morphism that uses `Math.random()` \'97 it will fail the law check because `f(f(x))` is not deterministic). That is the point: the laws are not constraints, they are **diagnostics** that tell you when your system is well-behaved enough to compose safely.\
