\
### Why you should care (in your terms)\
\
You work with complex-valued neural networks, Langevin dynamics, and quantum systems. In each of these, you are composing maps:\
\
- Complex layers: `\uc0\u8450 \u8319  \u8594  \u8450 \u7504 `\
- Langevin steps: `state_t \uc0\u8594  state_\{t+1\}`\
- Quantum channels: `\uc0\u961  \u8594  \u934 (\u961 )`\
\
Category theory asks: **do these compositions respect the laws?** If yes, every theorem about composition (functoriality, adjunctions, limits) applies to your system for free. If no, you have found a bug \'97 your system will break in ways that are hard to debug but easy to predict from the category.\
\
That is the diagnostic power. It turns "why does my network train?" from an empirical question into a structural one.\
